course: dictionary
lesson: 4375
title: A word from the ocean and the poem
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have one meaning in science and another in poetry. Gyre is one of them. «Each big ocean has huge circular currents called gyres» gives the science meaning: a gyre is a huge circle of ocean currents that moves round and round.
explain: The clues are in the words around it. «Huge circular currents» tells you the shape, and «plastic gets trapped in their calm middle» tells you the currents turn in a big loop, not a little whirl.
explain: Poets use gyre as a verb meaning to spin or whirl round. Lewis Carroll wrote «did gyre and gimble in the wabe», so the word there is an action, not a thing you can see on a map.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. «The rubbish is trapped in the calm middle of a huge circle of currents» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A gyre is a huge circle of ocean currents that moves round and round.
fact: Gyre can also be a verb in poetry, meaning to spin or whirl round.
fact: There are five big ocean gyres, and plastic rubbish collects in them, such as the Great Pacific Garbage Patch.
fact: In the northern hemisphere the currents of a gyre turn clockwise, and in the southern hemisphere they turn anticlockwise.
fact: W. B. Yeats wrote of «the widening gyre», using gyre to mean a spiral.
exercise: «Each big ocean has huge circular currents called gyres.» What is a gyre in this sentence? | A tall wave on the beach | A small fish that swims in the middle | *A huge circle of ocean currents that moves round and round | A type of plastic bottle
exercise: In the poem «did gyre and gimble in the wabe», what does gyre mean? | *To spin or whirl round | To shout very loudly | To sleep under a tree | To freeze into ice
exercise: Find and copy the word from «Each big ocean has huge circular currents called gyres» that describes the shape of the currents. | called | ocean | *circular | huge
test: «Plastic rubbish gets trapped in their calm middle.» Where does the plastic get trapped? | Under the desert sand | Inside a volcano | *In the calm middle of a gyre | In the middle of a football pitch
test: W. B. Yeats wrote of «the widening gyre». Which meaning fits best? | *A spiral that grows wider | A small, still pond | A loud drum beat | A hot, dry wind
exam: Which sentence uses gyre as a verb? | *The leaves gyre round and round in the wind | The ocean has five big gyres | The rubbish sat in the middle of a gyre | The gyre was full of floating plastic
